    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Urban landscapes offer a new and different ecosystem to organisms living in them, fostering different communities from neighboring natural areas and affecting the behavior of some organisms. Ants can be useful to urban ecology studies but are relatively understudied in this sphere. This thesis examines urban ants in two different ways. The first chapter compares seasonal foraging activity of a native ant, Liometopum occidentale, in urban and natural landscapes. I analyzed foraging activity at colonies in Naturalized Woodland, Urban Woodland, and Urban landscapes using rate counts and qualitative trail maps throughout the year. Results indicate that foraging activity is affected by season and an interaction between season and habitat urbanization type. Overall highest activity level occurred in the Spring, and lowest occurred in the Summer. During the Winter, Naturalized Woodland sites had higher activity than the colonies in other habitat urbanization types. During the late Summer, Urban colonies had higher activity than the colonies in other habitat urbanization types. This indicates a behavioral response to the different environmental conditions found in urban areas. The second chapter compares characteristics of native urban park oaks utilized by native and invasive ants. Of all ant species found in the surveys, the only invasive ant was Linepithema humile, the Argentine ant. Size and groundcover of oaks were found to be correlated with indigeneity of ants. Native ants were on significantly larger oaks than Linepithema humile and were disproportionately more likely than Linepithema humile to be on oaks with leaf litter groundcover, whereas Linepithema humile was disproportionately more likely than native ants to be on oaks with grass groundcover. Broadly, this thesis helps us understand how landscape influences behavior and how native ants occupy urbanized landscapes. These results have implications for behavioral ecology and urban planning.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
